uded from your gross income, meaning you pay income taxes on them upfront in the year you contribute. Nonqualified distributions are split pro rata between contributions and earnings, making a portion of early withdrawals taxable. Once contributed, designated Roth contributions cannot be recharacterized as pretax contributions. Many plans place employer matching contributions in a separate pretax account. Ask your plan administrator whether your plan allows Roth matching contributions. Your withdrawals are also subject to employer plan distribution restrictions."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the new IRA withdrawal rules for 2026?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The IRS pages we checked on November 18, 2026 describe higher 2026 contribution limits, not new withdrawal rules. IR-2025-111 raised the IRA contribution limit to $7,500 and the 401(k) employee deferral limit to $24,500. Withdrawals from Roth IRAs and designated Roth accounts still follow the five-year and age tests described above."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which is better for me, a Roth IRA or a 401(k)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A designated Roth 401(k) allows much higher annual contributions and has no modified adjusted gross income limits, meaning high earners can contribute directly regardless of their income level. A Roth IRA offers more flexible nonqualified withdrawal ordering rules because the IRS treats your regular contributions as coming out before earnings. You can explore how these features compare on our Roth IRA vs Roth 401(k) guide to match each structure to your current income and retirement savings goals."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a Roth 401(k) have required minimum distributions?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, designated Roth accounts in a 401(k) plan do not have required minimum distributions while the account owner is alive. The IRS required minimum distributions FAQs confirm that RMD rules do not apply to Roth IRAs or designated Roth accounts during the owner's lifetime. However, RMD rules do apply to beneficiaries who inherit a designated Roth account."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I roll a Roth 401(k) into a Roth IRA?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, you can roll an eligible rollover distribution from a designated Roth 401(k) directly into a Roth IRA. A direct rollover moves your balance between custodians and is reported on Form 1099-R using letter code H, meaning it is not a taxable distribution to you. A direct rollover is not taxed when it happens, and later withdrawals follow the Roth IRA's own five-year period and ordering rules."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is this tax advice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, this guide provides general federal tax information based on IRS publications and guidelines checked on November 18, 2026, and does not constitute formal tax or legal advice. Individual employer plan documents control what distributions your plan allows. A tax professional can apply this information to your specific return."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/","jobTitle":"Personal Finance Writer & Editor","description":"Jonathan Velez writes and edits ModernWallet's personal-finance content, focused on loans, interest, and debt payoff. Every calculator page is reviewed against primary sources like the CFPB and Federal Reserve.","knowsAbout":["Auto loans","Loan amortization","Interest and APR","Debt payoff strategies","Personal finance"],"worksFor":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com"}}</script> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Roth 401(k) Withdrawal Rules: Five-Year Clock and Taxes","description":"Understand Roth 401(k) withdrawal rules: qualified tax-free distributions, the five-year clock, pro rata earnings taxes, rollovers, and RMD requirements.","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/guides/roth-401k-withdrawal-rules/","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"},"datePublished":"2026-11-18","dateModified":"2026-11-18","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"}},"citation":[{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Retirement topics: Designated Roth account","url":"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-designated-roth-account"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS required minimum distributions FAQs","url":"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-plan-and-ira-required-minimum-distributions-faqs"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Publication 590-A, Contributions to Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590a"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Publication 590-B, Distributions from Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Instructions for Form 8606 (tax year 2025)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8606"}]}</script> <main class="page" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a href="/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>ModernWallet</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <a href="/guides/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Guides</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <span class="crumb-current" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Roth 401(k) Withdrawal Rules: Five-Year Clock and Taxes</span> </nav> <header class="hero" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h1 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Roth 401(k) Withdrawal Rules: Five-Year Clock and Taxes</h1> <div class="byline" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> <span class="by" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>By <a href="/about/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Jonathan Velez</a>, Personal Finance Writer &amp; Editor</span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <span class="rev" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 18, 2026</span> </div>   <p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Withdrawals from a Roth 401(k) are completely tax-free only when taken as a qualified distribution. To qualify, your withdrawal must occur at least five years after the year of your first designated Roth contribution, and you must also be at least age 59½, disabled, or deceased.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>If you take a nonqualified distribution instead, the Internal Revenue Service (IRS) taxes a pro rata share of your earnings and may add an early withdrawal penalty.</p> </header> <section class="tools" aria-label="Tools for this journey" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <span class="tools-title" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Tools for this journey</span> <div class="tool-pills" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a class="tool-pill" href="/roth-vs-traditional-401k-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Roth vs traditional 401(k)<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a><a class="tool-pill" href="/investing/roth-ira-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Roth IRA<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a><a class="tool-pill" href="/tax-bracket-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Tax bracket<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a> </div> </section>   <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>When a Roth 401(k) Withdrawal Is Tax-Free</h2> <p>A distribution from a designated Roth account in a 401(k) plan is excluded from your gross income only if it satisfies the federal definition of a qualified distribution. The <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-designated-roth-account" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Retirement topics: Designated Roth account</a> page outlines two separate hurdles that you must clear at the same time.</p><p>First, the distribution must satisfy the five-year aging test. This requirement means the payment occurs at least five years after the year of your first designated Roth contribution, counting that first year as part of the five. Second, the distribution must satisfy one of three life event conditions: it must be made on or after the date you reach age 59½, made to your beneficiary or estate on or after your death, or attributable to your being disabled.</p><p>When both requirements are satisfied, every dollar that leaves the account is fully tax-free. You pay zero federal income tax on your original contributions, and you pay zero tax on all accrued investment earnings. If you fail either requirement, your distribution is nonqualified, and the investment earnings portion becomes taxable income.</p><p>Your employer plan terms also dictate when withdrawals are permitted during employment. Even if federal tax rules define when money comes out tax-free, employer plan distribution restrictions determine whether you can take money out while still working. For other general retirement plan withdrawal mechanics, you can read our guide on <a href="/guides/how-to-withdraw-from-401k/">how to withdraw from a 401(k)</a>.</p><p>The table below details how the IRS treats different designated Roth account distributions based on your age, account age, and distribution method.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="prose-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Distribution Situation</th><th scope="col">Five-Year Test Met</th><th scope="col">Age 59½, Disabled, or Deceased</th><th scope="col">Federal Tax and Penalty Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Qualified distribution</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Entire withdrawal of contributions and earnings is tax-free and penalty-free</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Nonqualified distribution</th><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>earnings portion is taxable income; the 10 percent additional tax does not apply because the age, disability or death condition is met</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Nonqualified early distribution</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>earnings are taxable income and the 10 percent additional tax may apply unless an exception covers it</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Nonqualified early distribution</th><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>earnings are taxable income and the 10 percent additional tax may apply unless an exception covers it</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Direct rollover to Roth IRA</th><td>Not required</td><td>Not required</td><td>Not a taxable distribution to you. Later withdrawals follow the Roth IRA's own five-year period and ordering rules</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Direct rollover to designated Roth</th><td>Not required</td><td>Not required</td><td>Not a taxable distribution to you. Moves directly to another eligible plan</td></tr></tbody></table></div> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>The Five-Year Rule for Roth 401(k) Accounts</h2> <p>The five-year aging period for a designated Roth 401(k) is measured from the year of your first designated Roth contribution to that plan, counting that first year as part of the five. The IRS counts that first calendar year as year one of the five-year requirement, regardless of which month your initial payroll contribution occurred.</p><p>To see how this works, consider a worker who makes their first designated Roth contribution to a 401(k) plan in November of Year 1. Because the IRS counts the first year as part of the five, Year 1 counts as the entire first year. Year 2 is the second year, Year 3 is the third year, Year 4 is the fourth year, and Year 5 is the fifth year.</p><p>This workplace clock operates under different rules than an individual retirement arrangement. Under <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Publication 590-B</a> and the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8606" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Instructions for Form 8606</a>, the five-year period for a Roth IRA begins with the first tax year for which a contribution was made to a Roth IRA set up for your benefit. For a Roth IRA, that initial contribution can be a regular annual contribution, a conversion, or a rollover from a workplace retirement plan.</p><p>If you are comparing both account structures, you can read our comparison of a <a href="/compare/roth-401k-vs-traditional-401k/">Roth 401(k) vs Traditional 401(k)</a> or evaluate <a href="/compare/roth-ira-vs-roth-401k/">Roth IRA vs Roth 401(k)</a> accounts to understand contribution rules.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>What Happens With a Nonqualified Withdrawal</h2> <p>When you take a nonqualified distribution from a designated Roth 401(k), the IRS requires the payment to come out on a pro rata basis from contributions and earnings. This designated Roth rule differs sharply from Roth IRA rules, where regular contributions are considered distributed first.</p><p>In our internal calculation, we split a $10,000 nonqualified withdrawal using the IRS pro rata rule for an account holding $80,000 in contributions and $20,000 in earnings. Assuming an account composition of 80 percent contributions and 20 percent earnings, $2,000 is included in income. The $8,000 portion representing your original contributions is returned tax-free because you already paid income tax on those dollars when you earned them.</p><p>The 10 percent additional tax on early distributions may also apply to the taxable earnings portion. On a $10,000 nonqualified distribution with 20 percent earnings, the 10 percent additional tax applies only to the $2,000 earnings slice. That results in up to $200 in additional federal tax.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="prose-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Nonqualified withdrawal of $10,000 from an account that is 20% earnings</th><th scope="col">Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Included in income (earnings share)</th><td>$2,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Not taxed (contributions share)</th><td>$8,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">10% additional tax if it applies and no exception covers it</th><td>up to $200</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The exact tax hit always mirrors the balance ratio in your account. In an edge case where an account has experienced zero investment growth, the earnings share is zero. A $10,000 nonqualified withdrawal from an account holding only contributions results in $0 included in taxable income and $0 in additional tax. In the opposite edge case where an account consists entirely of investment growth, 100 percent of the distribution is included in gross income.</p><p>Many savers mistake the Roth 401(k) withdrawal mechanism for the Roth IRA distribution ordering rule. According to IRS Publication 590-B, nonqualified distributions from a Roth IRA follow a specific ordering sequence: regular contributions come out first completely tax-free, followed by conversion and rollover contributions on a first-in, first-out basis, and earnings come out last. In a designated Roth 401(k), you cannot withdraw only your contributions.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Rolling a Roth 401(k) Into a Roth IRA</h2> <p>You can move an eligible rollover distribution from a designated Roth 401(k) into another designated Roth account or directly into a Roth IRA. As stated in <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590a" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Publication 590-A</a>, a rollover from a designated Roth account can only be made to another designated Roth account or to a Roth IRA. It cannot be rolled into a traditional pretax IRA.</p><p>A direct rollover moves the funds directly into your Roth IRA without a taxable distribution to you. Under IRS reporting rules in Publication 590-B, a direct rollover of a designated Roth account distribution to a Roth IRA is reported on Form 1099-R using letter code H in box 7.</p><p>When a nonqualified distribution is paid to you and you roll over only a portion within 60 days, the IRS applies a special ordering rule to the rollover amount. Under this rule, any partial rollover of a nonqualified distribution is treated as consisting first of the untaxed earnings portion. The IRS demonstrates this mechanism in its published Joe example.</p><p>In the official IRS example, Joe receives a $14,000 nonqualified distribution consisting of $11,000 in contributions and $3,000 in earnings. Joe rolls $7,000 into a Roth IRA within 60 days. The IRS treats the $7,000 rollover as consisting of the full $3,000 in earnings plus $4,000 of contributions. Because all $3,000 of earnings entered the Roth IRA, none of the distribution is taxable to Joe.</p><p>In our calculated counterexample, consider what occurs if Joe rolls over only $2,000 of that same $14,000 distribution. Because the partial rollover absorbs earnings first, the $2,000 rolled into the Roth IRA covers only $2,000 of the $3,000 total earnings. That leaves $1,000 of earnings sitting in Joe's hands, which must be included in his gross income and may face the 10 percent additional tax. Rolling over at least the earnings portion protects the nonqualified distribution from immediate tax.</p><p>Once the money lands inside your Roth IRA, the Roth IRA rules govern future distributions. As outlined in Publication 590-B and Form 8606 instructions, the Roth IRA applies its own five-year aging period and its own ordering rules. Conversions from traditional accounts follow distinct schedules, which you can review in our guide on <a href="/guides/roth-conversion-rules/">Roth conversion rules</a> or when executing a <a href="/guides/backdoor-roth-ira/">backdoor Roth IRA</a>.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Roth 401(k) Required Minimum Distributions</h2> <p>Required minimum distributions do not apply to designated Roth accounts while the original account owner is alive. The official <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-plan-and-ira-required-minimum-distributions-faqs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS required minimum distributions FAQs</a> state that withdrawals from Roth IRAs and designated Roth accounts in 401(k) or 403(b) plans are not required until after the death of the account owner.</p><p>Under federal tax guidance, the RMD rules do not apply to Roth IRAs or designated Roth accounts while the owner is living. This means you are not forced to take distributions from your designated Roth account while you are alive.</p><p>However, required minimum distribution rules do apply to beneficiaries of both Roth IRAs and designated Roth accounts.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Our Takeaway</h2> <p>Wait until your designated Roth account is at least five years old and you have reached age 59½ or become disabled to take Roth 401(k) money tax-free. If you pull money out before clearing both hurdles, you should expect an earnings share to be included in your gross income and potentially assessed a 10 percent additional tax. If you leave your employer, a direct rollover to a Roth IRA is not a taxable distribution and moves the balance out of the employer plan's distribution restrictions, but the Roth IRA's own five-year period then applies to withdrawals.</p><p>Our recommendations follow the IRS designated Roth account page. Our internal calculation covers only the $10,000 example above. Several factors would change our answer for your situation: plan-specific distribution restrictions set by your employer, a qualifying disability or death, an existing Roth IRA that has already established its own five-year clock, or a new IRS rule. Check the first-contribution year on your plan statements, then compare your age and the account's age against the table to follow the Roth 401(k) withdrawal rules accurately.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="faqs" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is a Roth 401(k) tax-free when you withdraw?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>A Roth 401(k) withdrawal is completely tax-free only if it meets the requirements for a qualified distribution. Under IRS rules, you must hold the account for at least five years starting from the first year you contributed, and you must be at least age 59½, disabled, or deceased. If you take a nonqualified distribution before meeting both conditions, the earnings portion is included in your gross income and may incur an additional 10 percent early distribution tax.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>What is the downside of a Roth 401(k)?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>The primary downside of a designated Roth 401(k) is that your contributions are not excluded from your gross income, meaning you pay income taxes on them upfront in the year you contribute. Nonqualified distributions are split pro rata between contributions and earnings, making a portion of early withdrawals taxable. Once contributed, designated Roth contributions cannot be recharacterized as pretax contributions. Many plans place employer matching contributions in a separate pretax account. Ask your plan administrator whether your plan allows Roth matching contributions. Your withdrawals are also subject to employer plan distribution restrictions.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>What are the new IRA withdrawal rules for 2026?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>The IRS pages we checked on November 18, 2026 describe higher 2026 contribution limits, not new withdrawal rules. IR-2025-111 raised the IRA contribution limit to $7,500 and the 401(k) employee deferral limit to $24,500. Withdrawals from Roth IRAs and designated Roth accounts still follow the five-year and age tests described above.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Which is better for me, a Roth IRA or a 401(k)?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>A designated Roth 401(k) allows much higher annual contributions and has no modified adjusted gross income limits, meaning high earners can contribute directly regardless of their income level. A Roth IRA offers more flexible nonqualified withdrawal ordering rules because the IRS treats your regular contributions as coming out before earnings. You can explore how these features compare on our <a href="/compare/roth-ira-vs-roth-401k/">Roth IRA vs Roth 401(k)</a> guide to match each structure to your current income and retirement savings goals.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Does a Roth 401(k) have required minimum distributions?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No, designated Roth accounts in a 401(k) plan do not have required minimum distributions while the account owner is alive. The IRS required minimum distributions FAQs confirm that RMD rules do not apply to Roth IRAs or designated Roth accounts during the owner's lifetime. However, RMD rules do apply to beneficiaries who inherit a designated Roth account.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Can I roll a Roth 401(k) into a Roth IRA?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Yes, you can roll an eligible rollover distribution from a designated Roth 401(k) directly into a Roth IRA. A direct rollover moves your balance between custodians and is reported on Form 1099-R using letter code H, meaning it is not a taxable distribution to you. A direct rollover is not taxed when it happens, and later withdrawals follow the Roth IRA's own five-year period and ordering rules.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is this tax advice?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No, this guide provides general federal tax information based on IRS publications and guidelines checked on November 18, 2026, and does not constitute formal tax or legal advice. Individual employer plan documents control what distributions your plan allows. A tax professional can apply this information to your specific return.</p> </details> </div> </section>  <section class="section sources" data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x><h2 data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x>Sources</h2><p class="source-note" data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x>
